A||||4||Robert||Sunroof leaking problem||||5||
Z||000000||Robert||03-23-2000||11:48 PM||||So I just finished washing my 87GT, thought I would take is out for a high speed dry off.  I back out of my driveway and a stream of water lands in my lap. Is there a way to fix this?  The wet spot is somewhat embarrassing.<P>Thanks<BR>Robert<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Robert||03-23-2000||11:55 PM||||Opps!!!   I see now the Monkeyman has the same topic going.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||03-24-2000||12:42 AM||||99% of sunroof leaks are caused by the seals around the mounting bolts in the glass panel (for the latch mechanism and the hinge tabs) going bad.  New bolts with integral seals are still available from Pontiac.  I replaced all of mine for around $13.00.  I also replaced the hinge tabs because the plastic coating had come off of mine and let the glass panel rattle when closed.  They cost around $12.00 at the dealer also.<P>Another leak sorce is the actual sunroof opening gasket (but usually not the cause).<P>Check to see if yours is sealing against the glass by closing a piece of paper between the glass and the seal and then try to slip it out.  If it comes out easily at any point it is not making good contact with the glass.  Replacing worn out hinge tabs often fixes this.  You can also install a spacer between the roof panel and the latch mechanism to make it pull down harder when closed.  The latch mechanism is bolted to the roof panel.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||03-24-2000||08:26 AM||||'86 and up had drainage holes.  The Grape Ape ('85 GT) doesn't and it leaks bad.  Used the sunroof panel from the Monkeymobile ('86 2m4).  It has new "hinges" and washers.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||03-24-2000||08:57 AM||||the sunroof parts list can be found here.<P><A HREF="http://www.PONTIACFIERO.com" TARGET=_blank>Pontiac Fiero Knowledge Base</A>||||reg||1
